                <h1 class="content-title">Server-Side Power: <span class="gradient-text">Business Rules &amp; Script Includes</span></h1>
                <p class="content-subtitle">
                    Master the backend logic engines of ServiceNow — database trigger timings (before, after, async, display), 
                    modular Script Includes, the GlideAjax client-to-server bridge, and high-performance GlideRecord querying.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Section 1: Server Concept -->
            <section class="content-section" id="server-concept">
                <h2>The Server-Side Philosophy (The Kitchen Master Chef)</h2>
                <p>
                    While client scripts control what users see on their screens, <strong>Server-Side Scripts</strong> 
                    hold total authority over the database:
                </p>

                <div class="feature-grid">
                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>Direct Database Access</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            Server scripts execute securely inside ServiceNow's cloud application nodes. 
                            They have unrestricted access to read, insert, update, and delete database records across all tables.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="feature-card">
                        <div class="feature-card-header">
                            <h4>The Ultimate Security Gate</h4>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            A clever user might disable JavaScript in their browser to bypass a client script, 
                            but they can <strong>never bypass a Server-Side Business Rule</strong>.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 2: Business Rules -->
            <section class="content-section" id="business-rules">
                <h2>Business Rules (The 4 Execution Timings)</h2>
                <p>
                    A <strong>Business Rule (BR)</strong> is server-side JavaScript that executes automatically whenever 
                    a record is queried, inserted, updated, or deleted.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Every Business Rule runs at one of <strong>4 distinct execution timings</strong>:
                </p>

                <table class="data-table">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Timing</th>
                            <th>When It Runs</th>
                            <th>Database State</th>
                            <th>Best Used For</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>before</code></td>
                            <td>Runs <em>before</em> data is committed to SQL database.</td>
                            <td>Values are in memory on the <code>current</code> object.</td>
                            <td>Calculating field values (e.g. setting Assignment Group) or aborting invalid saves (<code>current.setAbortAction(true)</code>).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>after</code></td>
                            <td>Runs <em>immediately after</em> data is saved to SQL database.</td>
                            <td>Record is permanently committed to database.</td>
                            <td>Cascading updates to related records (e.g. closing child tasks when the parent incident resolves).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>async</code></td>
                            <td>Runs in the background task queue (asynchronously via <code>sys_trigger</code>).</td>
                            <td>Record is already committed; runs seconds later.</td>
                            <td>Sending outbound REST webhooks, heavy integrations, or mass email notifications without slowing down the user.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>display</code></td>
                            <td>Runs while assembling record data to send to the user's browser.</td>
                            <td>Read-only gathering phase.</td>
                            <td>Passing server data to the client form via the <code>g_scratchpad</code> object.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 3: The Fatal Anti-Pattern -->
            <section class="content-section" id="br-antipattern">
                <h2>The Fatal Anti-Pattern: Never Call `current.update()` in a BR</h2>
                <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                    <div class="info-card-title">⚠️ The Infinite Loop Crash Trap</div>
                    <p>
                        Calling <code>current.update()</code> inside a Business Rule that triggers on update tells the server: 
                        <em>"Hey, update this record again!"</em> $\rightarrow$ which triggers the Business Rule again $\rightarrow$ 
                        which calls <code>current.update()</code> again $\rightarrow$ creating an <strong>infinite recursive loop</strong> 
                        that crashes server memory!
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block">
                    <div class="code-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Correct vs Incorrect Syntax)</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// ❌ WRONG: Causes infinite loop recursion!
current.short_description = 'Updated text';
current.update(); 

// ✅ CORRECT (in a 'before' Business Rule):
// Simply assign the value! ServiceNow automatically commits current to the database.
current.short_description = 'Updated text';</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 4: Script Includes -->
            <section class="content-section" id="script-includes">
                <h2>Script Includes (Modular, Reusable Cookbooks)</h2>
                <p>
                    Instead of copying and pasting the same 50 lines of complex query code across 15 different Business Rules, 
                    developers write <strong>Script Includes</strong> (stored in <code>sys_script_include</code>).
                </p>
                <p>
                    A Script Include is a reusable JavaScript class or library that can be called from any server script or API:
                </p>

                <div class="code-block">
                    <div class="code-header">
                        <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Script Include: IncidentManagerHelper)</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>var IncidentManagerHelper = Class.create();
IncidentManagerHelper.prototype = {
    initialize: function() {
        // Constructor logic
    },

    // Reusable method to check if a caller is a high-priority executive
    isExecutiveVIP: function(userSysId) {
        var userGR = new GlideRecord('sys_user');
        if (userGR.get(userSysId)) {
            return userGR.getValue('vip') == '1' || userGR.getValue('title').indexOf('Director') !== -1;
        }
        return false;
    },

    type: 'IncidentManagerHelper'
};</code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 5: GlideAjax -->
            <section class="content-section" id="glide-ajax">
                <h2>GlideAjax (The Client-to-Server Bridge)</h2>
                <p>
                    When a user is filling out a form in their browser and needs data from the server (e.g. selecting a Caller 
                    and automatically pulling their Department and Manager Phone Number), developers use <strong>GlideAjax</strong>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    GlideAjax communicates asynchronously behind the scenes without refreshing the page:
                </p>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-ajax-server">1. Server Script Include</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-ajax-client">2. Client Script (Browser)</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-ajax-server">
                        <h3>Server: Script Include (Extends AbstractAjaxProcessor)</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Server Script Include)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>var UserInfoAjax = Class.create();
UserInfoAjax.prototype = Object.extendsObject(AbstractAjaxProcessor, {

    getUserDetails: function() {
        var userId = this.getParameter('sysparm_user_id');
        var userGR = new GlideRecord('sys_user');
        
        if (userGR.get(userId)) {
            var result = {
                email: userGR.getValue('email'),
                phone: userGR.getValue('phone'),
                department: userGR.getDisplayValue('department')
            };
            return JSON.stringify(result);
        }
        return null;
    },

    type: 'UserInfoAjax'
});</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-ajax-client">
                        <h3>Client: onChange Client Script</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript (Client Script)</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>function onChange(control, oldValue, newValue, isLoading, isTemplate) {
    if (isLoading || newValue === '') return;

    var ga = new GlideAjax('UserInfoAjax');
    ga.addParam('sysparm_name', 'getUserDetails');
    ga.addParam('sysparm_user_id', newValue);
    
    // Asynchronous non-blocking callback
    ga.getXMLAnswer(function(response) {
        if (response) {
            var data = JSON.parse(response);
            g_form.setValue('u_contact_email', data.email);
            g_form.setValue('u_contact_phone', data.phone);
            g_form.addInfoMessage('Loaded profile for ' + data.department);
        }
    });
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 6: GlideRecord Basics -->
            <section class="content-section" id="gliderecord-basics">
                <h2>GlideRecord &amp; GlideAggregate Basics</h2>
                <p>
                    <code>GlideRecord</code> is ServiceNow's native JavaScript Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) API 
                    for interacting with database tables:
                </p>

                <div class="tabs-container">
                    <div class="tabs-header">
                        <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-gr-query">Query Records</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-gr-insert">Insert / Update</button>
                        <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-gr-aggregate">High-Speed Aggregate</button>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-gr-query">
                        <h3>Querying Records with GlideRecord</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>var incGR = new GlideRecord('incident');
incGR.addQuery('active', true);
incGR.addQuery('priority', 1);
incGR.orderByDesc('sys_created_on');
incGR.setLimit(10);
incGR.query();

while (incGR.next()) {
    gs.info('Critical Incident: ' + incGR.getValue('number') + ' - ' + incGR.getValue('short_description'));
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-gr-insert">
                        <h3>Inserting &amp; Updating Records</h3>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Inserting a brand new record
var newInc = new GlideRecord('incident');
newInc.initialize();
newInc.setValue('short_description', 'VPN Gateway Outage');
newInc.setValue('urgency', 1);
newInc.setValue('impact', 1);
var newSysId = newInc.insert(); // Returns 32-character sys_id

// Updating an existing record
var updateGR = new GlideRecord('incident');
if (updateGR.get(newSysId)) {
    updateGR.setValue('work_notes', 'Assigned network engineering team for RCA.');
    updateGR.update();
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-gr-aggregate">
                        <h3>Counting Records with GlideAggregate (10x Faster)</h3>
                        <p>
                            Never use <code>GlideRecord.getRowCount()</code> to count thousands of rows because it loads every record into server RAM. 
                            Always use <code>GlideAggregate</code> to run native SQL <code>COUNT(*)</code> queries:
                        </p>
                        <div class="code-block">
                            <div class="code-header">
                                <span class="code-lang">JavaScript</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>var ga = new GlideAggregate('incident');
ga.addQuery('active', true);
ga.addAggregate('COUNT');
ga.query();

if (ga.next()) {
    var count = ga.getAggregate('COUNT');
    gs.info('Total Active Incidents: ' + count);
}</code></pre>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

